reviews.text_detailed_comments:
The framing of the problem is reasonably novel, and I particularly appreciate the effort to reformulate MR-WSI diagnosis as the question of when high-resolution evidence is actually needed. At the same time, although the paper is careful experimentally and shows clear advantages in both performance and practical efficiency, I think the current version still needs to be strengthened in several aspects if it is to be a more secure MICCAI acceptance:
1.further explain the rationale for mixing LrCS sigmoid evidence and HrFD softmax prediction during training;
2.provide more detailed implementation information for all baselines, verify reproduction quality against the official results reported in the original papers, add fair comparisons with the latest SOTA methods on BRACS, and clearly state the full experimental protocol, including dataset splits, feature extraction, and patch extraction, so that the results are easier to trust and reproduce;
3.provide independent ablations for the structure-aware graph module and the structure-level distillation loss, to better support the necessity and advantage of the core design choices;
4.fix the remaining minor writing and formatting issues in the manuscript. 
Overall, this is a fairly strong WSI-MIL methodology paper with clear novelty and practical value, although there is still some room for improvement.

My main reason for recommending Weak Accept is that the central motivation of the paper is valid, and it is clearly more thoughtful than a straightforward multi-resolution fusion design. In particular, the idea that high-resolution information should not be used unconditionally once the low-resolution evidence is already sufficient is both intuitively appealing and consistent with how pathologists actually work in a coarse-to-fine manner. The framing is therefore quite attractive. At the same time, the quantitative results are strong across three datasets, and the experimental analysis is more complete than what is usually seen in borderline submissions. I believe the paper offers a meaningful new perspective for MR-MIL.
The manuscript still has some weaknesses, but I do not see them as fatal. The paper would benefit from a stronger explanation of the mixed-supervision design, clearer and more transparent baseline reproduction details, and additional ablations for some of the key structural modules. Still, these issues do not undermine the main contribution of the work. In my opinion, this is a solid MR-MIL methodology paper with clear novelty and clinical relevance, and it has real acceptance potential for MICCAI. For that reason, I recommend Weak Accept, with the final decision depending on the quality of the rebuttal and the completeness of the authors’ clarifications.

