author_feedback_text:
We thank the reviewers for the constructive feedback. We respectly respond as follows.
@R1-Q2, R4-Q1 Main contribution. 
We clarify that our key innovation is not a general policy optimization algorithm, but a diagnosis-localization-verification reasoning framework for CT tumor report generation. The model first performs a global diagnosis on the full CT and predicts a key slice for local reasoning. And we introduce a novel cross-view consistency reward to encourage reliable evidence localization. Accordingly, the main contributions are: (1) extend one-shot report generation to two-stage reasoning. (2) enforce global-local consistency via key-slice re-query. (3) shift the optimization target from text to evidence-grounded correctness, mitigating hallucination.
@R2-Q1, R4-Q3, R1-Q1457 The distinct roles and implementation details of SFT and RL. 
Our results show that general-purpose models(e.g. Qwen, commercial models), perform poorly on tumor tasks, making SFT necessary for domain-specific initialization and explaining the gain from 53.55 to 83.57.However, SFT mainly fits the text distribution and lacks interpretability and reliability. Our evidence-based RL improves lesion-attribute correctness and evidence-grounding, which are more clinically relevant to radiologists. On attribute VQA(Tab 2), our E-MRL improves over SFT from 57.49 to 67.53(+17.5%). In implementation, we built ~9.2K single-organ SFT samples from AbdomenAtlas3.0 with mask-derived key-slice supervision. For RL, we select about 2K hard cases with incorrect attribute predictions for targeted optimization. Training on 16 H20 GPUs takes about 12h for SFT and 14h for RL.
@R1-Q3, R2-Q3, R4-Q1 Related work and our novelty. 
Thank you for suggestions and we will add the missing citations. The key difference of our work is that we use VLM’s intermediate reasoning to localize the key slice, rather than relying on preprocessed suspicious regions(e.g. segmentation masks). Moreover, our rewards supervise the outcome, reasoning process, and verification simultaneously. Most prior methods either lack explicit grounding(e.g. MedVLM-R1, Med3D-R1, VALOR), or rely on preprocessed suspicious regions as auxiliary input(e.g. MedReason-R1). GF-Screen optimizes pan-cancer screening, which differs from our task.
@R1-Q6, R4-Q2, R5-Q1 Evaluation protocol, Qwen-235B judge, and RadGPT references. There is no evaluation circularity and the GT annotations are clinically rigorous. Qwen-235B is used only as a tumor-attribute parser(e.g., size, location, enhancement), not a direct evaluator. In Tab 1, sen., spec., and B-Acc are computed from parsed tumor existence. In Tab 2, the same parser is applied to both model outputs and GT reports to extract attributes for fair comparison. In addition, a radiologist on our team manually reviewed 10% of test set and confirmed 100% agreement with the parsed attributes. Regarding R4’s concern about RadGPT reference, the original RadGPT paper(ICCV2025) states that the GT, i.e., “RadGPT-generated report”, was revised and confirmed by 12 board-certified radiologists, ensuring high clinical quality.
@R2-Q2, R4-Q4 3D terminology, reproducibility details, and presentation.
Our task is defined on 3D volumetric CT, while the input is formatted as a video sequence to match backbone. We will clarify this wording to avoid confusion. We will also add more implementation details. Concretely, our RL uses GRPO for 5 epochs with AdamW(lr 5e-6), gradient accumulation 8, group size 8, and KL coefficient β = 0.01.Full preprocessing and model configurations will be released in code repository.
@R5-Q2Q3 Failure mode and key slice selection.
We analyzed the failure cases and found that common failure cases include very small tumors(<1 cm), multiple co-existing tumors, and abnormal enhancement under poor CT quality. We also acknowledge possible annotation variance in mask-based key-slice supervision and will add this discussion in the final version.

