reviews.text_strengths:
1.Problem–method alignment is stronger.
In MRI, the signal is already complex-valued, and data are acquired in k-space. Each Gabor primitive’s Fourier transform (Eq. 2) is a Gaussian blob centered at the modulation frequency $\boldsymbol{\xi}
n$ with a position-dependent phase. Standard Gaussian primitives, regardless of spatial position, always have spectra centered at the k-space origin (spatial translation introduces phase shift, not frequency shift), which is a particularly severe limitation in MRI where high-frequency k-space regions encode tissue boundaries and other clinically important structures. The modulation frequency $\boldsymbol{\xi}_n$ precisely fills this gap.
2.The two-component low-rank temporal model is physically motivated and well-designed.
Equations (4)–(5) decompose temporal variation into a geometry bases (capturing in-plane cardiac motion via position, scale, rotation, and frequency perturbations) and an signal-intensity bases (capturing through-plane motion-induced signal changes). The coupling term $c_n^{[w]} \cdot \mathbf{v}
{g,t}$ in Eq. (5), which links primitive weights to the geometry basis, models motion-induced intensity effects such as partial-volume and flow. This reflects genuine understanding of cardiac cine physics rather than being a purely mathematical convenience.

