author_feedback_text:
We sincerely thank reviewers for their valuable comments and recognition of our work as “compelling”, “novel” and “a strong contribution to MICCAI”. All typos and unclear descriptions have been revised and will be reflected in the next version.
@Meta Reviewer @R3Q1Q2 Core Idea and Loss Design.
Re: The core attribution of this paper is to propose a novel harmless verification threat model. Unlike traditional methods that verify ownership through malicious behaviors, we prevent harmful behaviors by keeping watermarked labels identical to the originals, using prediction accuracy for verification. To implement this verification feasibility, we design a loss function driven by two objectives to optimize the watermark.
1)To ensure the watermark remains imperceptible, the visual loss combines MSE and SSIM to keep watermarked and original reconstructed images visually consistent.
2)To ensure effectively verification, the CLIP loss uses cosine similarity to distinguish features between clean and watermarked samples, while the CE loss optimizes the watermark for easier classification by the surrogate model, leading to higher prediction accuracy.
Removing visual loss reduces PSNR (between the reconstructed images with and without watermark) by 27.28 dB. Removing CLIP loss reduces Verification Success Rate (VSR) by 15%. Removing CE loss reduces watermarked dataset prediction accuracy by 15.8% and lowers VSR by 10%.
@R1Q1 @R3Q4 Real-World Feasibility.
Re: Our method is evaluated on a large-scale dataset comprising 104 COVID and 56 normal cases, achieving 61.42/100(mean Benign Accuracy/Verification Success Rate). Although class imbalance reduces mBA compared to small-scale datasets, the 100% VSR confirms the watermark’s effectiveness. We expand the watermark into multiple channels to match consecutive slices, yielding 68.11/100(mBA/VSR).
In practical deployment, our watermark has low storage and simple embedding logic, enabling seamless integration through lightweight software or firmware updates. We are collaborating with hospitals and equipment vendors for future deployment.
@R1Q2 Generalization Under Aggressive Denoising Algorithms.
Re: To show that our feature-level watermark verification is independent of pixel-level denoising algorithms, we apply the watermark optimized via FBP to the protected dataset and use a pre-trained FBPConvNet during verification, yielding 69.35/100(mBA/VSR).
@R2Q1 Comparison with SOTA.
Re: Under the same setting as Table 1, EntropyMark(EM) achieves 57.55/50/91.77/91.38(mean BA/VSR/mC_b/mC_w). EM relies on a low-entropy patched set for verification, but patch-based methods like Badnet in our scenarios still produce high-entropy outputs, reducing verification performance.
@R2Q2Q3 @R3Q1 Experimental Setting.
Re: In Table 1, ResNet18 is our backbone model and the watermarking ratio is 0.2.We use the original CLIP model with ViT-B/32 architecture. The original CLIP model extracts general visual features to facilitate the feature-level separation between clean and watermarked samples, as confirmed by our ablation studies. Furthermore, to verify the generalizability of our method across different CLIP models, we evaluated an architecture-identical medical pre-trained model, yielding 69.76/100 (mean BA/VSR).
@R2Q2 Cross-Architecture Comparison.
Re: BadNet achieves 70.59/85 and 78.92/45 (mBA/VSR) on EfficientNet and MobileNet, respectively. FIBA\dagger achieves 68.26/65 and 77.65/70, while FIBA\ddagger achieves 70.61/85 and 76.99/65.
@R2Q3 @R3Q3 Clarifying Vague Terms.
Re: The learnable watermark is class-specific, where m_c denotes the watermark of the c-th class.
Measurement-domain data represent the intensity attenuation of X-rays passing through the object from different projection views.
We select SSIM as the fidelity metric because it emphasizes structural integrity critical for medical diagnosis [1].
[1] Structurally-sensitive multi-scale deep neural network for low-dose CT denoising.

