meta_reviews.text:
This paper proposes IC-MTL, an interaction-aware multi-task framework for joint thyroid nodule segmentation and malignancy classification, leveraging bidirectional cross-task interaction and contrastive regularization. The reviewers generally agree that the paper addresses a relevant and meaningful problem and presents a well-structured multi-task framework. Reviewer #1 and #2 is clearly positive, highlighting strong ablation studies and consistent gains, while Reviewer #3 is more cautious, mainly due to concerns about novelty and missing comparisons. The overall assessment lies around the weak accept borderline, with some disagreement on the strength of the contribution.
The main strengths are:
A clinically grounded formulation that explicitly models bidirectional interaction between segmentation and classification;
A coherent and well-motivated framework, combining cross-task interaction and contrastive learning;
Thorough ablation studies and qualitative analysis, supporting the contribution of individual components;
Evaluation on both a public dataset and an independent clinical dataset.
The main weaknesses include:
Limited novelty at the component level, with similarities to prior multi-task interaction mechanisms;
Missing comparisons with some relevant multi-task baselines and limited analysis of generalization.
Despite some limitations in novelty and evaluation scale, the paper presents a well-executed and clinically meaningful multi-task framework, with consistent empirical improvements and solid experimental support. The contribution is considered sufficient for acceptance, particularly given its relevance and methodological clarity.

