reviews.text_contribution:
The main contribution is a calibration-oriented survival learning framework for WSI MIL based on counting-process theory. Specifically, the paper proposes:
(1) A risk-conditional, time-local ACMT loss that enforces conditional moment orthogonality between residual increments and subgroup variables to improve calibration;
(2) A multi-slide ACMT objective to regularize prediction consistency across slides from the same patient;
(3) An empirical demonstration that the proposed training improves calibration and generalizes better under zero-shot cross-institution transfer compared to standard NLL-based MIL methods.

